/// send the tools?" - the question TotallyHot.ArcRouter's detection ladder asks first, and the one whose answer
/// decides whether emulation is even reached. The two are recorded separately because a single transcript
/// keyed by scenario cannot hold both, and because conflating them is how a model that silently ignores
/// tools gets mistaken for one that considered them and declined.
/// </para>
///
/// <para>
/// <b>Read the token counts, not the prose.</b> The assistant text on a dropped-tools reply looks like an
/// ordinary refusal - "I suggest getting online to get real-time information" - and is indistinguishable
/// by eye from a model that saw <c>get_time</c> and chose not to use it. <c>prompt_tokens</c> is what
/// separates them, and it is why whole envelopes are stored rather than message content.
/// </para>
/// </summary>
